 The numerical testing results obtained for different QIR models are consistent with this mathematical formulation. For each model and each test image, the error rate (ER) between the original (O) and encrypted (E) images is close to $100\%$, while the SSIM and correlation coefficient (CC) between O and E images are close to zero. The mean squared error (MSE) is very large, while the peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) is very low, indicating that the E images are visually unrecognisable and statistically very different from the O images. When O and decrypted (D) images are compared, all distortion measures vanish and all similarity measures reach their ideal values, indicating exact pixel-level decryption. Classical encryption metrics such as the NPCR, UACI, and entropy, defined as the measure of disorder based on probabilities, also show that the E images have strong confusion and diffusion and near-uniform grey-level distributions. In particular, NPCR is close to $99.6\%$, UACI is close to the ideal value of $33.33\%$, and entropy is close to $8$ bits per pixel.
